WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.329 --> 00:00:06.870
On March 6th, protestors gathered in San Francisco
to rally for peace in Ukraine.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.758
On the pavement beneath them, they painted
this image.

00:00:09.758 --> 00:00:15.356
A blue and white dove, wings outstretched,
surrounded by vibrant orange flowers.

00:00:15.356 --> 00:00:20.680
It’s a copy of a 1982 painting called 
“A Dove Has Spread Her Wings and Asks for Peace”

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.409
by Ukrainian artist Maria Prymachenko

00:00:23.409 --> 00:00:26.875
a self-taught painter known for her 
colorful, joyous scenes

00:00:26.875 --> 00:00:29.743
incorporating themes from Ukrainian folk traditions

00:00:29.743 --> 00:00:32.974
who Picasso once called an “artistic miracle.”

00:00:34.061 --> 00:00:37.370
The week prior, posts on social media showed
a small museum

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.830
that housed her paintings burning to the ground.

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:45.287
The fire destroyed, by some accounts, 
up to 25 major works.

00:00:45.287 --> 00:00:48.979
Satellite images show that none of the surrounding
structures were damaged

00:00:48.979 --> 00:00:53.660
indicating an intentional, targeted 
attack by Russian forces.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.280
It was one of the first examples 
of what experts fear

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.500
will be devastating damage to Ukraine's physical
culture.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.470
It's one thing to try and destroy the intellectual
and emotional part of our culture

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:06.770
but it's another thing to destroy the actual
physical representations of that.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:11.430
All the things that define us as a community,
as a country, as a nation

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.130
that's what we are fighting for.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:16.470
It’s a fight that’s played out countless
times around the world.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:21.212
Across history people have risked their lives
to save art like this from war zones.

00:01:21.212 --> 00:01:22.948
So how do they do it?

00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:23.877
And...

00:01:23.877 --> 00:01:26.341
What do we stand to lose if they fail?

00:01:30.532 --> 00:01:35.000
World War II was a turning point in the history
of cultural heritage preservation.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.788
Systematic Nazi looting,
book and art burning

00:01:37.788 --> 00:01:40.650
and the sheer destructive force 
of modern weaponry

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.422
put the world’s artistic treasures 
at unprecedented risk.

00:01:44.422 --> 00:01:50.210
Which prompted an unprecedented response.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:54.340
Britain's national gallery whisked their collection
away to a Welsh mine.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:57.670
American art historians formed 
a roving military unit

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:01.520
to essentially re-steal art from Nazis.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.220
And all over Europe, soldiers and volunteers
worked to shield masterpieces

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.304
using bricks, sandbags, and scaffolds.

00:02:09.304 --> 00:02:11.260
Which worked pretty well.

00:02:11.961 --> 00:02:15.232
Da Vinci’s "Last Supper" survived an accidental
bombing in Milan.

00:02:15.232 --> 00:02:18.293
Thanks to the scaffolding that bolstered 
the wall it’s painted on.

00:02:18.293 --> 00:02:22.225
And the sandbags and wood that absorbed 
some of the blast.

00:02:22.225 --> 00:02:25.262
But direct attacks are another story.

00:02:25.262 --> 00:02:27.577
Like in the German city of Dresden.

00:02:27.577 --> 00:02:32.509
Where targeted Allied bombing wiped out
90% of the city’s historic center.

00:02:36.332 --> 00:02:40.069
After the war, to try to prevent future damage,
the international community

00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:42.950
signed the 1954 Hague convention.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:49.268
Which requires them to protect cultural property
in the event of armed conflict

00:02:49.268 --> 00:02:52.840
and it includes provisions or articles that require them

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.428
you know, not to target cultural heritage.

00:02:55.428 --> 00:02:59.033
Dr. Bassett is the Director of the Cultural Heritage Monitoring Lab.

00:02:59.033 --> 00:03:04.866
A group that uses high-res satellite imagery
to track threats and damage to sites globally.

00:03:04.866 --> 00:03:08.070
Partially, as evidence for future prosecution.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.401
Because under the 1954 convention,

00:03:10.401 --> 00:03:15.437
“damage to cultural property means damage
to the cultural heritage of all mankind”.

00:03:15.437 --> 00:03:20.412
So attacks on cultural heritage 
are a considered war crime.

00:03:20.412 --> 00:03:23.390
But treaties can only do so much.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:25.681
In the years since, conflicts around the world

00:03:25.681 --> 00:03:29.135
have rendered immeasurable damage to cultural heritage.

00:03:29.135 --> 00:03:31.032
A lot of it intentional.

00:03:31.032 --> 00:03:33.917
Like the Taliban’s destruction 
of the Bamiyan buddhas.

00:03:33.917 --> 00:03:37.488
And Isis’ attacks on ancient sites all over Syria.

00:03:37.488 --> 00:03:40.352
That cultural heritage is not only impacted,

00:03:40.352 --> 00:03:45.110
but in many ways it's implicated and 
central to armed conflict.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:50.234
These are things that people point to that
are unifying factors for their society.

00:03:50.234 --> 00:03:53.095
They are tangible reflections of their identity.

00:03:53.095 --> 00:03:58.540
And Putin has made it clear that identity
is at the ideological center of Russia's invasion.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:01.905
[in Russian] “I would like to emphasize
again that Ukraine is not just

00:04:01.905 --> 00:04:03.773
a neighboring country for us.

00:04:03.773 --> 00:04:07.037
It is an inalienable part of our own history,

00:04:07.037 --> 00:04:10.000
culture, and spiritual space.”

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.849
He thinks that we don't really exist

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:16.030
and they want to destroy all the signs of
our identity.

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:19.289
Vasyl Mytsko and his colleagues at the Lviv
Gallery of Art

00:04:19.289 --> 00:04:23.749
oversee tens of thousands cultural items 
around Ukraine

00:04:23.749 --> 00:04:27.354
and that's still only a fraction of what the
country stands to lose.

00:04:29.774 --> 00:04:32.819
The first step in protecting cultural heritage

00:04:32.819 --> 00:04:35.507
is identifying what needs protecting.

00:04:35.507 --> 00:04:40.152
The 1954 Hague Convention requires that each
country keep an inventory

00:04:40.152 --> 00:04:42.081
of their cultural heritage sites.

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:43.780
But, that’s a big ask.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:47.550
I will say that the U.S. does not have a list
like that.

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:49.362
Ukraine didn't either.

00:04:49.362 --> 00:04:53.770
But Dr. Bassett and his team started at the
first sign of Russian escalation.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:59.331
We started building our mapping inventory
for Ukraine in April of 2021

00:04:59.331 --> 00:05:01.632
well before the invasion this year.

00:05:01.632 --> 00:05:04.582
The final inventory looks something like this.

00:05:04.582 --> 00:05:08.834
Each of the 20,000 plus blue dots 
is a different site.

00:05:08.834 --> 00:05:12.289
The country is home to 7 UNESCO world heritage sites

00:05:12.289 --> 00:05:16.276
including the entire historical center of Lviv.

00:05:16.276 --> 00:05:20.400
And the Saint Sophia's Cathedral, Saint Sophia
Square, one of the most beautiful places in

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:24.025
Ukraine and my personal favorite place in
Ukraine.

00:05:24.025 --> 00:05:27.309
Caterina Buchatskiy is the cofounder of the
Shadows Project

00:05:27.309 --> 00:05:30.479
a group dedicated to preserving and promoting
Ukrainian culture.

00:05:30.479 --> 00:05:33.199
We have Baroque, we have modernism,
we have neoclassical

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:36.110
all on the same street and it all works together
and it creates

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:40.361
this beautiful visual mosaic of all the
different Ukraines that have existed.

00:05:40.361 --> 00:05:44.391
Plus historically significant buildings, archaeological
sites, monuments

00:05:44.391 --> 00:05:46.360
and, of course, museums.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.876
Featuring works by Ukrainian artists like
Kazimir Malevich.

00:05:49.876 --> 00:05:52.650
An array of traditional Ukrainian folk art

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:58.297
And troves of scythian gold, ancient jewelry
from what is now Crimea.

00:05:58.297 --> 00:06:01.810
Which Russia has an established history of
attempting to loot.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:07.024
Though, the international community has ruled
they rightfully belong to Ukrainian.

00:06:07.024 --> 00:06:10.575
But after everything is cataloged, it has
to be secured.

00:06:10.575 --> 00:06:13.945
The 1954 convention also requires that each country 

00:06:13.945 --> 00:06:17.242
have a group dedicated to safeguarding cultural heritage.

00:06:17.242 --> 00:06:22.534
It is very much a constellation of government
agencies, militaries

00:06:22.534 --> 00:06:28.409
NGOs, academics, museum institutions that
are honestly working collaboratively

00:06:28.409 --> 00:06:30.979
to collectively do this type of work.

00:06:30.979 --> 00:06:33.689
When the invasion began museums had to scramble

00:06:33.689 --> 00:06:38.130
to make their own inventories, secure their
buildings, and move items to safety.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.233
Even in cities under siege

00:06:40.233 --> 00:06:44.669
volunteers and workers return day after day 
to safeguard Ukraine's treasures.

00:06:44.669 --> 00:06:48.632
I'm actually here in Lviv right now, where
I just dropped off the first round of shipments

00:06:48.632 --> 00:06:50.376
to the National Museum in Lviv.

00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:53.978
We dropped off fireproof equipment 
and we have some generators 

00:06:53.978 --> 00:06:56.118
and fireproof cabinets coming in next week.

00:06:56.118 --> 00:06:59.712
As much as we can do to minimize collateral
damage, we are doing.

00:06:59.712 --> 00:07:04.671
Days before the country was invaded, the ministry
of culture sent out private guidance to museums.

00:07:04.671 --> 00:07:07.129
And has since asked that the international
community 

00:07:07.129 --> 00:07:10.340
withhold information about the whereabouts 
of their collections. 

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:12.569
If history is any indication

00:07:12.569 --> 00:07:16.228
collections have moved underground 
or outside of major cities

00:07:16.228 --> 00:07:18.140
or out of the country entirely.

00:07:19.156 --> 00:07:25.526
And for some of Ukraine’s immovable treasures,
history is repeating itself more directly.

00:07:25.526 --> 00:07:29.212
In Odesa, guards once again stand watch 
over the opera house

00:07:29.212 --> 00:07:32.247
backed by sandbags and roadblocks.

00:07:32.247 --> 00:07:36.969
And in Lviv, volunteers are covering stained
glass windows with metal panels

00:07:36.969 --> 00:07:41.316
and roving the city wrapping statues with
tarps, insulation, duct tape

00:07:41.316 --> 00:07:43.760
whatever materials they can muster.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:49.309
In the hopes that, like before, 
it’ll be enough.

00:07:49.309 --> 00:07:51.169
Let's say it this way.

00:07:51.169 --> 00:07:54.159
We hope for the best but are preparing for
the worst.

00:07:54.159 --> 00:07:59.605
The starting place for all of this work is
knowing that cultural heritage will be lost.

00:07:59.605 --> 00:08:03.753
Dr. Bassett and his team also monitor 
impacts to sites.

00:08:03.753 --> 00:08:06.909
So far, they’ve registered hundreds.

00:08:06.909 --> 00:08:09.149
Early on, the Ukrainian cultural ministry

00:08:09.149 --> 00:08:13.238
launched a site to collect evidence of crimes
against cultural heritage

00:08:13.238 --> 00:08:15.689
organizing it all in a timeline.

00:08:15.689 --> 00:08:19.531
To use as evidence for potentially prosecuting
war crimes.

00:08:19.531 --> 00:08:25.888
And as a way to organize future efforts to
secure and restore whatever is damaged.

00:08:25.888 --> 00:08:30.105
After the war ends, I don't want to be looking
around at empty walls.

00:08:30.105 --> 00:08:31.888
I want to be seeing myself.

00:08:31.888 --> 00:08:34.580
I want to be seeing my people reflected along
with me

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.349
and I want to make sure that not only my people
today are surviving

00:08:38.349 --> 00:08:42.390
but all of our ancestors before us are surviving
with us via their art.

